"reviews\\\":[{\\\"actor\\\":\\\"wren\\\",\\\"decision\\\":\\\"develop\\\",\\\"ability\\\":null,\\\"reason\\\":\\\"The retrieved hand-lash-crossing schema supports adjacent binding turns and an overhand closure, but has no argument or mechanism for transverse frapping. Its trials and inspection are reusable, not equivalents of tightening the turn bundle. Related binding, cord-making and joining capabilities were checked; none supplies the missing function. Send a general frapping operation to the existing current-conversation development handoff. The recovered 0.64 m cord is not presumed long enough: the previous 0.6124 m assembly requirement leaves only 0.0276 m relative to that previous route, not a verified frapping allowance. Development waiting advances no world time and grants neither a lashing nor mastery.\\\",\\\"specification\\\":\\\"Develop Hand-frap crossing lashings, parameterized by bindingId or a proposed crossing described by pieceIds, cordId, crossingAngle and wrapCount; frapCount 1-3; modes assess, attempt, inspect, trial, untie and cancel. Minimal supported mechanism: transverse turns made with the existing continuous cord, between the crossed pieces and around the binding-turn bundle, followed by the existing overhand closure. Separate frapping cords and joined-cord routing are outside this version; report that limitation without inventing materials. The operation is applicable to any actor meeting the physical and study prerequisites, not just Wren.\\\\nReferences and reuse: call or extract the actual planner, closure, ownership, stage, trial and wear implementations of hand-lash-crossing@1 / multi-turn-contact@1, hand-bind-crossing / crossing-contact@1, fiber-cord@1, cord-knot@1 and cord-bend@1. Record these dependencies and the code reused in the implementation manifest. Retain existing handler ownership of ordinary binding construction; do not duplicate it or claim its reinvention. Add a versioned transverse-contact law and extend trial geometry to use its resulting turn tensions. Existing unfrapped bindings must retain their old behavior. hand-join-fiber-cords does not authorize treating a joined cord as admissible input.\\\\nPrerequisites: independently completed study of this operation; an existing compatible hand-lash-crossing binding for attempt, or owned admissible pieces and cord for assessment. Building the base lashing uses hand-lash-crossing and its independently learned prerequisite, actual costs and physical execution. Assessment may preview that base route without building it. Require reach, usable hands, dry footing and the same dry clear workspace, hand-clearance, lifting and bystander exclusions as hand-lash-crossing. Revalidate exact ownership, condition, availability and geometry before each stage. No other participant acts or communicates automatically.\\\\nWren's supplied candidate references are branches item-295-wren-3384 and item-287-wren-3309 and recovered cord item-337-wren-3866; check current state at execution rather than assuming paged records are complete. There is currently no tied lashing to modify. The shorter cords item-303-wren-3473, item-250-wren-2847 and item-226-wren-2532 are not automatically reserved, combined or substituted. No harvesting, longer cord, extra stock or unseen resource information is granted.\\\\nCosts: study 3 personally paid rounds at 0.2 energy each. Assess: one round at 0.5 energy, performing actual positioning, route measurement and clearance checking; it does not tie a lashing. Inspect: one round at 0.2 energy. Attempt on an existing compatible binding: frapCount + 3 paid rounds at 0.5 energy each: measure and plan, release the original closure while supporting the turns, route and snug each transverse turn, then close/dress/release-check. Attempt includes its own current measurement even after a prior assessment. Reserve the two pieces and cord by exact IDs, with no material consumption or duplication. A measured shortfall stops after the paid measurement stage, before undoing the binding. Every successful paid continuation executes only its next stage. Trial: one round at 0.5 energy, requested force greater than zero and at most 10 N, duration greater than zero and at most 60 seconds, direction slide or separate. Full untie: frapCount + wrapCount + 1 paid rounds at 0.5 energy, undoing closure, transverse turns and base turns; preserve existing condition and jam restrictions. No tools, water, heat or consumables are supplied.\\\\nGeometry and conservation: evaluate the actual turn bundle, bend radii, available gap, hand access and collision-free transverse path between the pieces. Account for each transverse centreline, travel from the base turns to the transverse route and back to the closure, finite cord width, successive-turn offsets, closure formation, tails and transient hand-passing slack. Required length is the maximum across all assembly stages, not just the finished route and not the old need plus an assumed allowance. Reuse the existing 0.04 m passing slack and closure/tail rules where applicable. Store the measured route and each stage's required length; report the limiting stage and numerical shortfall when one is physically measurable. Reject intersecting routes and routes below the cord bend limit. If clearance or length is insufficient, leave unchanged items and report that outcome, not a completed frap. A feasible precursor is this paid, informative assessment even if no complete frap fits.\\\\nMechanical implementation: the new law must deterministically compute contact paths, per-turn tension and transferred squeeze from the actual geometry. Snugging uses the existing bounded 5 N hand pull, material friction, grip, curvature, breaking and creep rules; propagate tension loss at each cord-on-cord and cord-on-bark contact, use unilateral contact with no compressive cord tension, and prevent overlap or unexplained shortening. Transverse work can take up actual slack and change normal forces; it does not assign an arbitrary strength bonus or multiply the old slip threshold. Derive trial resistance from the resulting contact forces using the inherited friction rules, with closure retention, migration, bend weakening, damage and breakage checked at each stage. If the required coupled contact calculation is not yet implemented or cannot resolve a configuration, report unresolved mechanics and retain a nonvalidated work state; never report improved retention from appearance alone. Implement these established checks in code, with regression tests for zero slack, insufficient length, blocked gap, slipping closure and broken cord; do not require repeated model review to execute them.\\\\nProducts and evidence: retain the original pieces and cord as conserved items linked by a versioned lashing relation, not additional material. Track length allocation, original and transverse routes, closure, tension, slack, migration, condition and fragments. Finished means only that the release check held under its specified conditions. Trials record requested and actually applied force, direction, duration, relative displacement, creep, slip, damage and breakage. A held separation test is not a sliding rating. Wren's earlier 10 N for 60 seconds and 4.3 N sliding slip remain historical evidence, not guaranteed current capacities. No safe working load, suspended-load use, shelter or universal strength claim follows.\\\\nLifecycle: persist workId, exact item references, paid stages, geometry revision and current physical state. Continue only on actual paid actor action; low energy, absent action, lost reach or blocked workspace pauses without charge. Recheck the route on resume. Unsupported hand-held turns relax on interruption; completed paid work remains recorded, but any physical rerouting or redressing needed after relaxation must be performed and paid, never silently restored. Cancel is immediate and free, releases unused reservations once and leaves the actual loose or tied work in place; it does not magically undo turns. Death releases reservations once at the actual location and lets hand-held work relax, preserving tied parts, damage and fragments. No paid time, energy or wear is refunded. Unworked material remains recoverable; breakage creates conserved fragments, never a replacement cord. Bound-cord wear remains 0.0005 condition per elapsed world round, applied once per cord despite multiple relations, with existing creep and load rules; study/review waiting does not run this process. No automatic retensioning or indefinite trial load persists. Maintenance requires inspection and actual paid redressing or untying; deteriorated or jammed work follows the inherited restrictions.\\\",\\\"learning\\\":3,\\\"experienceReview\\\":\\\"Separate functional retention from experience. Requesting, searching, registration, study completion and naming produce no pleasure or satisfaction. This first version grants no automatic mood benefit for tying, inspecting or trialing; ordinary nutrition, energy expenditure and physical comfort are unchanged. Actual assessment or trial may generate verified personal information, including an informative length or clearance failure, only for the actor who performed and observed it. Deduplicate by material/geometry/condition, tested direction, force-duration envelope and measured result, not by action name, workId or new capability ID. Recording information does not itself credit a protected reward account. Any later experience hook must use real personally applicable events with an explicit cap, decay and cooldown; none is silently added here. Physical comfort cannot exceed 10, and nutrition is never reduced by mood. Do not modify reward accounts, budget, goals or permissions.\\\",\\\"visualReview\\\":\\\"Implement reusable visual metadata for binding and work events: item references, workId/bindingId, stage, crossing angle, base and transverse centreline paths, turn counts, closure state, slack, migration, condition and evidence status. Emit observable measurement, closure-opened, turn-routed, turn-snugged, release-check, trial-start, displacement/slip/break, pause, resume, cancel and untie events only when they actually occur. Assessment may display a clearly distinguished measured route overlay and numerical shortfall, never a completed frapped binding. Render partial or relaxed turns as partial or relaxed, unresolved mechanics as unvalidated, and fragments only after actual breakage. A completed appearance does not imply improved strength; display held only with the exact observed direction, force and duration. Do not show celebratory success or benefit effects for failed, incomplete, merely requested or untested work, and disclose no hidden stock or unobserved material properties.\\\"}]}\"}","annotations":[],"logprobs":[]}],"phase":"final_answer"}],"error":null,"incomplete_details":null,"tools":[],"tool_choice":"auto","parallel_tool_calls":true,"prompt_cache_key":"186afd8b-6076-4443-b7cd-5ee175e098f7","prompt_cache_retention":"24h","text":{"format":{"type":"json_schema","name":"bounded_decision","description":null,"schema":{"type":"object","additionalProperties":false,"required":["queries","result_json"],"properties":{"queries":{"type":"array","maxItems":4,"items":{"type":"object","additionalProperties":false,"required":["section","search","ids","offset","part"],"properties":{"section":{"type":"string","enum":["abilities","inventory","knowledge","projects","memory","state","observed"]},"search":{"type":"string","maxLength":400},"ids":{"type":"array","maxItems":4,"items":{"type":"string","maxLength":240}},"offset":{"type":"integer","minimum":0},"part":{"type":"integer","minimum":0}}}},"result_json":{"type":["string","null"]}}},"strict":true},"verbosity":"medium"},"top_p":0.98,"top_logprobs":0,"temperature":1.0,"reasoning":{"context":"all_turns","effort":"medium","summary":null,"mode":"standard"},"user":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":11016,"input_tokens_details":{